# tests/rib

## sphere.rib

Written for phase 0's runtime baseline. Not third-party.

## quadrics.rib

Phase 4, step 3. All seven RenderMan quadrics -- Sphere, Cone, Cylinder,
Hyperboloid, Paraboloid, Torus, Disk -- in one scene, each exercising its own
`getLocation`/`getNormal` parameterization end to end (tessellation, shading,
silhouette), not only the isolated finite-difference proof
`tests/normals_test.cpp` already covers. Not third-party.

## screenwindow.rib

Phase 4, step 3. `RiScreenWindow`, `RiCropWindow` and `RiFrameAspectRatio`
together under perspective: an asymmetric `ScreenWindow` shifting the visible
frustum off-centre, then `CropWindow` rendering only a sub-window of that
already-shifted result -- the combination the UAT-defect regression fixtures
(`orthowindow.rib`, `cropwindow_test.cpp`) test in isolation but not together.
`FrameAspectRatio` has no tokenizer entry; included here to prove it is
warned-and-skipped, not a parse desync. Not third-party.

## clipping.rib

Phase 4, step 3. A cylinder parallel to the camera's z axis, far longer than
the scene's `[near,far]` `Clipping` range, seen in profile: `GMANPolygonClipper`
must cut it cleanly at both ends, producing a finite visible band rather than
the whole tube, a degenerate sliver, or a crash. Not third-party.

## shaders.rib

Phase 4, step 3. The three shipped surface shaders -- matte, plastic, metal --
side by side under identical lighting, so a regression confined to one
shader's own math shows up as a change confined to its own sphere. Not
third-party.

## patch_norender.rib

Phase 6. A `"bilinear"` `Patch` beside a control `Sphere` under identical
lighting and shading -- once the defect `tests/patchnorender_test.cpp` pins
(`Patch` rasterized no pixels), fixed when `getRSPatch` was wired to
`createParametric`. The `Sphere` proves the renderer itself works and the
pixels are the `Patch`'s own. No golden minted -- bilinear interpolation of
a flat four-point patch has no shading detail worth pixel-regression
protection. Not third-party.

## patch_bicubic.rib

Phase 6. A `"bicubic"` `Patch`, under `Sides 1` and a lit `"Kd"`: the
point set is the same whether the sixteen-point control net is read in
RISpec's u-fastest/v-major order or transposed, so `Sides 2` or `Ka`-only
shading cannot tell them apart -- a transposed reading culls to nothing
here instead. A trailing `Sphere` follows AGENTS.md's "RIB authoring"
section, "The desync convention." Not third-party.

## patchmesh_bilinear.rib

`PatchMesh "bilinear"`, nonperiodic on both axes: nu=3 nv=2, two patches
side by side sharing their middle column of control points -- the
smallest grid that exercises sub-patch selection rather than degenerating
to a single `Patch "bilinear"`. A trailing `Sphere` follows AGENTS.md's
"RIB authoring" section, "The desync convention." Not third-party.

## patchmesh_bilinear_periodic.rib

`PatchMesh "bilinear"`, periodic on both axes: an outer and inner diamond
ring of control points, each wrapping its last strip back to control
point 0 -- the case `createParametric` samples all the way to u=1.0 and
v=1.0, where a mesh's wraparound indexing is most likely to read out of
bounds. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not
third-party.

## patchmesh_bicubic.rib

`PatchMesh "bicubic"`, nonperiodic on both axes: nu=7 nv=4 under the
default Bezier basis, two sub-patches in u sharing their middle column.
Control points are evenly spaced and coplanar, so `GMANBasis::bicubicMesh`
must trace the same flat rectangle a bilinear mesh would; any curvature in
the render would mean a sub-patch boundary picked up the wrong control
points. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not
third-party.

## patchmesh_bicubic_periodic.rib

`PatchMesh "bicubic"`, periodic on both axes: nu=6 nv=3, three concentric
hexagonal control rings, exercising `GMANBasis::offset`'s wraparound on
both axes at once. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not
third-party.

## patchmesh_mixed_wrap.rib

`PatchMesh "bicubic"` with `uwrap "periodic" vwrap "nonperiodic"`: a
hexagonal tube, two periodic sub-patches around and one nonperiodic
sub-patch along its length. `uwrap` and `vwrap` are independent tokens --
the varying parameter count is (patches on the periodic axis) times
(patches+1 on the nonperiodic axis), not one axis's rule applied to both;
this is the only fixture that can tell that formula apart from a
collapsed all-or-nothing one, via an explicit `"Cs"` sized to the count
it implies. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not
third-party.

## malformed/patchmesh_baddim.rib

Syntactically well-formed, semantically invalid: nu=5 does not align to
the default Bezier basis's step of 3. Registered in
`tests/patchmesh_test.cpp`, not `tests/ribmalformed_test.cpp` -- that file
is scoped to tokenizer-level malformation and asserts only "does not
crash," neither of which this is. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## patchmesh_bspline_periodic.rib

`PatchMesh "bicubic"` under a `Basis "b-spline" 1 "b-spline" 1` (step=1),
periodic on u at nu=3 -- the tightened periodic floor
`validPeriodicBicubicMeshDim` requires at step=1 (every other bicubic
fixture here uses the default Bezier basis, step=3, where the old,
looser bound was already tight enough to be safe). Regression guard: the
fix must still accept and correctly render every periodic mesh a step=1
basis can legally form at its minimum. A trailing `Sphere` follows the
desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/patchmesh_bspline_periodic_baddim.rib

The step=1 counterpart to `malformed/patchmesh_baddim.rib`: nu=1
periodic under a `Basis "b-spline" 1 "b-spline" 1`, one below
`validPeriodicBicubicMeshDim`'s true floor at step=1. Against the
unfixed validator (`n>=step` alone) this read one point past a 4-point
`"P"` array -- `GMANBasis::offset`'s periodic wraparound is a single
`i-=nu`, not a true modulo, and only stays in range for nu>=3 at
step=1. Registered in `tests/patchmesh_test.cpp`, asserting the same
warn-and-`create()` fallback as `patchmesh_baddim.rib`. A trailing
`Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/generalpolygon_short_p.rib

`nverts` sums to more vertices than `"P"` holds -- `GeneralPolygon`'s own
short-`"P"` case, `patchmesh_short_p.rib`'s counterpart. Registered in
`tests/paramclamp_test.cpp`, asserting the same clamp-and-warn behaviour.
A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/generalpolygon_negative_nverts.rib

A negative `nverts` entry: `RiGeneralPolygonV` rejects it before sizing
the parameter list, warns naming the request and the offending value, and
adds the empty stub to the world instead of indexing `"P"` by a negative
count. Registered in `tests/paramclamp_test.cpp`. A trailing `Sphere`
follows the desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/generalpolygon_empty_nverts.rib

An empty `nverts` array -- `nloops` is its length, so this is `nloops = 0`.
Registered in `tests/paramclamp_test.cpp`, asserting the same
warn-and-`create()` fallback. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/patchmesh_zero_step_basis.rib

`Basis "b-spline" 0 "b-spline" 0` ahead of a bicubic `PatchMesh`:
`RiBasis` takes `ustep`/`vstep` on faith, and `RiPatchMeshV`'s
`nupatches`/`nvpatches` divide `nu`/`nv` by that step before
`getRSPatchMesh` ever validates it. Registered in
`tests/patchmesh_test.cpp`, asserting the same warn-and-`create()`
fallback as `patchmesh_baddim.rib`. A trailing `Sphere` follows the
desync convention. Not third-party.

## nupatch_bilinear.rib

An order-2x2 `NuPatch` (`nu = nv = 2`, knots `[0 0 1 1]` on both axes) --
a clamped degree-1 B-spline, mathematically bilinear interpolation of its
own four control points, the same as `patch_bilinear_twin.rib`'s
`Patch "bilinear"` over the same points in the same order. Registered in
`tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, rendered and compared pixel by pixel against its
own twin. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not
third-party.

## patch_bilinear_twin.rib

`nupatch_bilinear.rib`'s own twin: the same four control points in the
same order, issued as a plain `Patch "bilinear"`. Registered in
`tests/nupatch_test.cpp`. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## nupatch_texturecoordinates.rib

Pins the out-of-scope decision that `RiTextureCoordinates` never changes
a `NuPatch`'s render: `getRSNuPatch` always passes `kIdentityCorners`
regardless of what was supplied. An order-2x2 `NuPatch` over the same
camera, lighting, `Sides 2` and paintedplastic/checker-texture surface as
`tests/rib/texcoords/patch_texturecoordinates.rib`, carrying that
fixture's own corner transpose. Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`,
asserting this fixture and `nupatch_texturecoordinates_default.rib`
render bit-for-bit the same. Not third-party.

## nupatch_texturecoordinates_default.rib

`nupatch_texturecoordinates.rib`'s own twin, with no
`RiTextureCoordinates` at all. Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`.
Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_short_uknot.rib

`uknot` one value short of the declared `nu + uorder`. The RI signature
carries no knot-array length, so `parseNuPatch` checks it itself and
calls nothing rather than handing `RiNuPatchV` a short array. Registered
in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting the warn-and-return fallback. A
trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_decreasing_knots.rib

`uknot` is the declared length but not non-decreasing. Registered in
`tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting the same warn-and-`create()`
fallback as `nupatch_short_uknot.rib`. A trailing `Sphere` follows the
desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_order_exceeds_n.rib

`uorder = nu + 1`: two control points cannot support a cubic span.
Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting the same
warn-and-`create()` fallback. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_empty_range.rib

`umin == umax`. Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting the
same warn-and-`create()` fallback. A trailing `Sphere` follows the
desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_umin_below_knot.rib

`umin < uknot[uorder-1]`. Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`,
asserting the same warn-and-`create()` fallback. A trailing `Sphere`
follows the desync convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_short_pw.rib

`"Pw"` two values short of its declared `nu*nv*4`. Registered in
`tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting the existing `GMANParameterList`
clamp warning fires (before `RiNuPatchV`'s own weight-positivity check
ever runs). A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync convention. Not
third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_zero_weight.rib

A full-length `"Pw"` array with one control point's own weight `<= 0`.
Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting the new
weight-positivity warning. A trailing `Sphere` follows the desync
convention. Not third-party.

## malformed/nupatch_illegal_block.rib

A `NuPatch` issued inside `FrameBegin`/`FrameEnd` before any
`WorldBegin` -- the "F"-only block state `cmdNuPatch`'s mask excludes.
`allowed(cmdNuPatch)` throws before this task's own validation runs,
after `parseNuPatch`'s knot arrays are already owned and before its
trailing `delete[]`s, proving that ownership survives the throw.
Registered in `tests/nupatch_test.cpp`, asserting exit status 1 (not the
exit-0 shape every other `malformed/nupatch_*.rib` fixture takes) and no
trailing `Sphere`, since the throw unwinds before one would parse. Not
third-party.

## rotate.rib

An in-world `Rotate` must compose ahead of the transforms declared after it,
about the world origin, not the camera (`AGENTS.md`, "Handedness and matrix
convention"; `tests/rotate_test.cpp`). Not third-party.

## polygon.rib

Phase 5. A single convex pentagon (five vertices, not four -- a 4-vertex
fixture cannot distinguish `RiPolygonV`'s parameter-list sizing fix from
the bug it fixes) under one ambient light, so the raster coverage and the
shaded color both have an independently hand-computable value
(`tests/polygon_test.cpp`). `Clipping 0.5 50` per `AGENTS.md`'s RIB
authoring section: a camera-facing planar polygon is exactly the flat,
narrow-in-z geometry the near-clip precision defect hits hardest. Not
third-party.

## polygon_general_hole.rib

Written for the `GeneralPolygon` hole-bridging task: a square with a square
hole cut from its centre, `polygon_concave.rib`'s shape and camera. Not
third-party.

## malformed/

Hand-written fixtures for malformed RIB input. `gman` is expected to fail on
every one of these; the point in each case is *how* it fails -- cleanly, with
no leak and no hang, not with a crash or an infinite loop.

| File | Exercises |
|---|---|
| `display_badtype.rib` | `parseDisplay` copies `"name"` before it validates `"type"` -- a bad token in `"type"`'s place must not leak the already-copied `"name"`. |
| `stringarray.rib` | A string array with a non-string element, via `TokenVector::toRtTokenArray` -- must release the strings it already duplicated before throwing. |
| `truncated.rib` | A RIB stream cut off mid-keyword, no trailing newline -- the tokenizer's main read loops must terminate cleanly at end of input rather than looping or reading past the buffer. |
| `unbalanced_bracket.rib` | A bracketed array with no closing bracket -- `GMANRIBParse::parseArray` must stop at EOF rather than consuming the rest of the file looking for a `]` that never arrives. |
| `unterminated_string.rib` | A plain (non-array) string with no closing quote, running to EOF -- `GMANRIBTokenize::parseString` must terminate cleanly at end of input. |
| `declare_array_overflow.rib` | A `Declare` array-size digit string that overflows even `long` -- `GMANInlineParse::is_int` must reject it rather than let `strtol` saturate to a garbage quantity that sizes a later `new RtFloat[]`. Not tokenizer-level like the rest of this table; shares the harness because the bar is the same, see `tests/ribmalformed_test.cpp`. |

Each is run under a hard wall-clock timeout (`tests/ribmalformed_test.cpp`),
independent of ctest's own `TIMEOUT` property, since a tokenizer hang on
malformed input is exactly the failure a test-level timeout alone might not
catch cleanly.

## requests/

Minimal, hand-written fixtures, one per RISpec 3.2 request with
parse-and-ignore coverage (`ifelse.rib` covers the four conditional requests
together; `solid.rib` covers `SolidBegin`/`SolidEnd` together -- neither pair
is meaningful RIB in isolation). Most were added by phase 2;
`pixelfilter.rib` covers `PixelFilter`, the one request phase 2's own list
missed. `generalpolygon.rib` covers `GeneralPolygon`, a request with a
variable-length `nverts` array ahead of its parameter list -- the same
desync exposure a mis-counted parse anywhere else in the front end has.
Each follows AGENTS.md's
"RIB authoring" section, "The desync convention."

## nonewline/

Hand-written fixtures for the tokenizer's end-of-input defect: a token that
runs to end of input with no trailing delimiter had its last character
duplicated (`WorldEnd` became `WorldEndd`). `top.rib` is a top-level file
ending `WorldEnd` with no trailing newline; `parent.rib`/`child.rib` and
`parent_gz.rib`/`child.rib.gz` are the same defect through a plain and a
gzip'd `ReadArchive` target, `child.rib` itself ending `AttributeEnd` with no
trailing newline.

## corpus/

Real RIB from third-party software, fetched once and checked in so tests are
hermetic (no network at test time).

| File | Source | License | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `menger.rib` | [aqsis/aqsis](https://github.com/aqsis/aqsis) `examples/procedurals/menger/menger.rib`, commit `d258e4ee5200ef29a512f2f4237b06fa3e4bb853` | GPL-2.0 (repo license; this file itself carries no separate header) | The corpus test (`tests/ribdialect_test.cpp`). Shader-free, so it is one of the few Aqsis examples that reaches `RiWorldEnd` without hitting a missing shader plugin. Exercises `Procedural "DynamicLoad"` and the unbracketed `"fov" 45` parameter form. |
| `bike.rib`, `bikeData.rib.gz` | [aqsis/aqsis](https://github.com/aqsis/aqsis) `examples/features/archives/`, commit `a9ecc18a9ccbddb418b3ad3940c98a541e427461` (model courtesy of jrMan, www.jrman.org, per the file's own header) | GPL-2.0 (repo license) | Exercises `ReadArchive` of a gzip'd file in the same step, over ~5,300 lines of real `Patch`/`Basis`/`TransformBegin` content -- the two hardest step-6/step-7 paths together. Phase 3 ships a real `plastic` shader, closing the shading-infrastructure gap that used to stop this file at its first `Surface "plastic"` call. It used to die on `RIE_NESTING -- Blocks cannot overlap` further in -- `bikeData.rib.gz` ends `TransformEnd` with no trailing newline, which the tokenizer's end-of-input defect corrupted into `TransformEndd`, leaving that block unclosed (`tests/rib/nonewline/`'s defect). Fixed, `bike.rib` reaches exit 0 and writes a non-empty image; the image used to be blank (a separate, pre-existing defect -- `Patch` rasterized no pixels at all) until phase 6 wired `Patch` to `createParametric`, and now reads as a bicycle. Used for the dedicated `ReadArchive`/gzip proof, not the corpus proof. |

Checked in as gzip'd/plain exactly as fetched; `bikeData.rib.gz` is not
decompressed in-tree so the gzip-transparency test has a real gzip'd file to
exercise, not a synthetic one.
